



Sprint Retrospective and Review: Tangible Benefits of Agile in Our Workplace

Joshua Thomas Nash Simmons
Southern New Hampshire University
CS 250: Software Development Lifecycle
Professor Tammy Morrison
February 22, 2026
 
This first sprint retrospective and review assesses the changes in productivity and team health following Scrum Agile method adoption. The team consisted of a Scrum Master, Product Owner (PO), Developer, and Tester. Through the development of the SNHU Travel project, the team has provided significant evidence that the Agile work model provides tangible production benefits.
As the Scrum Master, I facilitated collaboration and task accomplishment by identifying barriers to both and providing solutions to prevent bottlenecks. One example, to ensure scrum meetings met time limits, we adopted a “speaking token” in the form of a blowup ball which encouraged engagement and minimized loss of focus. The Product Owner (PO) acted as an open channel between the team and the clients and stakeholders. The PO maintained a refined Backlog. Adaptive, but not unpredictable, the PO ensured the team had opportunities to provide feedback on client needs so the Backlog presented an actionable picture of feature priority and intended size. Feedback loops like this made the Sprint Planning and Backlog Refinement meeting more efficient and productive. The Developer worked efficiently on User Story completion and enforcement of our “Definition of Done”. An example was while developing the Travel History Profile (User Story ID: 2). The Developer noticed that the initial pass/fail measures implemented by the Tester didn’t align with her understanding of client expectations. Feedback from the PO resulted in minimal delays as a result. The Tester ensured feature rollout included robust and timely pass/fail metrics. A major part of our team’s “Definition of Done” relied on thorough testing to measure feature proximity to client intent. While developing the initial test cases for the Top Destinations by Budget feature (User Story ID: 6), the team provided feedback that the initial pass/fail measures were insubstantial and that fringe cases weren’t sufficiently accounted for and the Tester redesigned a more thorough test case. 
Fulfillment of these roles required removing myself, the Tester, and the PO from direct feature development. Although full scrum role separation is ideal, due to the small size of this team, there were a few instances where bottlenecking occurred due to only having one Developer and one Tester. Potential solutions for this could be either maintaining a 4 person team but embracing more fluid roles (each role is more collateral than primary) or increasing team size by incorporating more Developers and Testers. A single PO and Scrum Master would still be effective, but the missing production personnel would have a smaller impact. Although more like a traditional programmer than the PO or Scrum Master, the Tester is still a role that removes a potential contributor to feature development. However, by moving a potential developer to the Tester role, the team can exercise Test-Driven Development (TDD). TDD allows the formal testing process to occur in situ. Although this meant that feature completion hinged on adequate testing, TDD caught defects sooner and on a smaller scale and encouraged modularity.
The Backlog contained six User Stories to be completed during the sprint. Two User Stories were improperly sized and following feedback from the team were reassessed during Backlog Refinement. Further, 2 large-sized story adjustments occurred during development. Despite this, all six User Stories were fully developed, tested, and released. The Developer and Tester asked the PO probing, concise questions which in turn empowered the PO to do the same for our clients. In return, the PO provided a clear vision of each feature for the Developer and Tester to ensure done features meant meeting client needs and expectations. For example, while designing the Vacation Type Filter (User Story ID: 4), feedback from the stakeholders, delivered by the PO, changed the scope of the design by directing implementation of a specific “wellness” vacation focus. Immediately following this adjustment to the Backlog, the Developer and Tester provided split-second questions to the PO, who was able to return promptly with the answers the team needed to take that feature to done.
Early in the sprint, the team identified the importance of communication and throughout the sprint, this acknowledgement was proven true. For example, during our daily scrum meetings, the Developer was discussing a bottleneck occurring in coding the Price Filter (User Story ID: 3). The Tester was able to provide immediate feedback and together they overcame the bottleneck promptly, preventing undue interruption. We emphasized the need to keep email and message communication efficient and actionable. For example, while adjusting the Vacation Type Filter requirements to focus on “wellness”, the Developer inquired “Should the implementation of the wellness/detox-focused vacation packages be an additional filtering option (paired with the vacation type and price filter)?”. The PO’s answer to this provides actionable, precise data to the Developer to ensure features meet client intent.
During the sprint, we experienced a few setbacks that highlighted the shortcomings of Agile. Ceremonial overhead, fatigue, and requirement volatility were three areas that required proactive engagement to maintain sprint success. Fulfillment of the Scrum roles resulted in only one Developer and Tester which created capacity constraints. Because our work process relied so much on teamwork and communication, there were a few times where breakdowns or interruptions in communication caused the entire team to pause work. The focus on open dialogue led some team members to experience social fatigue. Further, although the flexibility was overall a benefit to our team’s production, the mid-process pivots in direction led to team stress and fatigue. Because we operated with a modular coding mindset, these pivots were absorbable. If our team had not been so proactive, these pivots would have caused low morale and delayed feature rollout. Ensuring success when adopting the agile mindset needs to be proactive to mitigate these drawbacks of agile. Any obstacles to timely and effective communication need to be identified and mitigated early. Assume production direction is going to change at the most inconvenient time and help teams promote a coding practice of abstraction and single responsibility to localize rework.
For the next sprint, we will adopt new methods to target the weaknesses from this sprint. There were instances where bottlenecking occurred because the Tester and Developer needed a prompt, formal adjustment to the Backlog, but the PO was unreachable late in the day. To mitigate this, we will be performing Backlog Refinement earlier and more often. Additionally, we will formally adopt the Affinity Grouping estimation process to ensure we minimize mis-sizing User Stories in future. These adjustments reflect a shift from reactive adaptation toward proactive sprint risk mitigation.
Despite adding the additional task of field testing the agile method as a company policy, the success of this first sprint is evidence that agile provides tangible benefits. 100% feature rollout. Multiple pivots in development direction mid-process. A Waterfall approach would have allowed all four team members to focus on feature development and required fewer formal ceremonies. However, it would also have delayed defect discovery and limited stakeholder feedback until later stages of development. The Scrum framework enabled earlier testing and iterative stakeholder input, allowing the Developer to adjust implementation during the sprint rather than after completion. This sprint provided significant evidence that the Scrum Agile method provides tangible benefits over the traditional Waterfall project method.
